WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.730
- And then once this green cell

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:10.330
the linker cell reaches
the tail of the animal,

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.000
it dies there

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.130
and it has to die there,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.810
because if it doesn't die,

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.090
it kind of clogs this connection

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.050
that the gonad makes with the cloacal

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.860
which allows the sperm
of the worm to release.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.250
So, essentially it will result

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.710
in the animal being
and fertile and sterile

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.040
so, essentially this is the second big job

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.030
of this linker of green
linker cell that it has to die

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.540
and the way it dies,
it's really interesting

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.490
and probably, you know, you look

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.110
you'll read the prime or the preview,

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.610
the review article

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.600
and there's a lot of
complicated information

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.953
but I hope you got from it

00:00:52.953 --> 00:00:55.830
that there are different
types of cell death

00:00:57.552 --> 00:01:00.660
and there was a little section
on linker cell death there,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.640
we will not go into all the details

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.830
but, I just kind, I just wanted to share

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:09.010
with you one observation
that really struck me

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:11.043
when I started to study this,

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.930
so, when the cell
migrates in the young worm

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.773
it has a very nice and round nucleus,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.380
so, it's a migrating linker cell here

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.960
but when it starts to die

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.950
what we see is that this
nucleus becomes quite deformed

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.363
and we call it nuclear deformation,

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.570
and so I was very curious to understand

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.480
how this deformation happened

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.350
from this nice and round nucleus,

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.380
like in all the normal cells

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.580
to this very deformed nucleus

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.810
and why is that important?

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.030
Actually, this different
kind of nucleus is observed

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:53.350
in all of the aggressive cancer types

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.490
and the more aggressive the cancer is

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.290
the more the deformed the nucleus.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.280
Also it's observed in some of
the neurodegenerative diseases

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.890
and the aging diseases

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:08.650
and the diseases of the
heart is cardiomyopathy is

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.990
in the muscular dystrophy

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.520
so, it seems to be relevant

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.580
to many, many different conditions

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.150
and I'm just really, really
interested to understand

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.340
how does this happen

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:25.510
and today with you people
tackle this first question

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:26.830
to understand

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.290
to sort of form an observation about this

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:36.290
and so what I wanted to do
is to look in live animals

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.290
and look specifically at the nucleus,

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.160
using some of the cell biology tools,

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.060
to see, how this change happens

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.500
and when step back for a second

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.560
I will just to show you a schematic

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:57.640
of the nucleus structure.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.770
So, this is the whole cell that you have

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:02.583
and this is the nucleus inside,

00:03:02.583 --> 00:03:05.420
then you have a DNA in here,

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.850
so, the nucleus has a double membrane

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.410
and you call it the nuclear envelope

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:14.200
and then there is this Lamina in the blue

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.790
which is composed of Lamin protein

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.690
and essentially what I
decided to do is to look

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.620
at this Lamin protein,

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:28.520
because it will allow me to
see how the nucleus looks

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.970
and how do we do with that?

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.710
So, that's the due, thanks
to this awesome technology

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:42.070
of the green fluorescent
protein called GFP

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:45.400
this protein was discovered in jellyfish

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.950
and there was actually a Nobel
prize was awarded in 2008

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.040
in chemistry to three
scientists who worked with us

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.100
and they saw that this protein

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.750
emits green light

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.330
when you shine UV light on it

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:06.130
and they were able to put it

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:09.060
attach it to other proteins and cells,

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.440
it's kinda like a piggyback

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.670
and so you can follow other proteins

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:18.340
and see what they do in
the cell or in the animal.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.440
So here, what I have is
I have my Lamin protein

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.420
and I have the GFP attached to it

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.630
