00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:17.910
and your thirties

00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:21.140
muscular dystrophy is where

00:32:21.140 --> 00:32:23.280
your muscles just weather a way

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:26.510
all of these kinds of diseases,

00:32:26.510 --> 00:32:31.020
so it's, I think it's a quite
light spectrum of diseases

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:32.550
and one of the things

00:32:33.917 --> 00:32:36.280
I'm trying to determine, you know,

00:32:36.280 --> 00:32:39.650
which direction to go with my research

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:43.040
or should I do a little bit of

00:32:43.040 --> 00:32:45.713
that connects to different diseases?

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:50.300
- And what got you into
wanting to study cell death

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:52.263
over other aspects of cell bio?

00:32:54.640 --> 00:32:58.930
- This, kind of came a
little bit from my PHD

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:03.930
my PHD was in this process
called DNA methylation

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:09.320
so when you have your
DNA and on the cytosines

00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:13.620
you are able to add the methyl group

00:33:14.496 --> 00:33:19.496
and for example, a big part
of our DNA that used to

00:33:19.610 --> 00:33:24.610
be called junk DNA has this
methylation mark on it.

00:33:24.680 --> 00:33:28.713
Also a huge part of our DNA

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:32.180
is composed of viral DNA

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:36.530
or viruses that in fact,
that us through, you know

00:33:36.530 --> 00:33:38.340
through all the ages

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:41.950
and could incorporate
that DNA into our DNA

00:33:41.950 --> 00:33:46.920
this was a mechanism for the
cell to tag the spiral DNA,

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:48.260
so it does not,

00:33:48.260 --> 00:33:50.950
so the cell knows this is not my DNA.

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:55.480
I can not touch this DNA and
the right to silence this DNA

00:33:56.440 --> 00:34:01.430
and there was just some
interesting observation that when

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:06.090
in these cells that are
deficient for DNA methylation

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:08.280
that they were dying in a wired way

00:34:10.218 --> 00:34:13.240
and not the right that we know,

00:34:13.240 --> 00:34:15.040
but the different kind of way

00:34:16.707 --> 00:34:21.160
and also some of it that was connected

00:34:21.160 --> 00:34:24.900
to different neuropathy diseases

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:27.300
and just kind of through this

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:29.280
I was curious to understand

00:34:30.200 --> 00:34:31.620
what happens with cell death

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:36.010
and I might go back to
these deficient methylation

00:34:36.010 --> 00:34:38.600
cells to see what happens there.

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:40.880
But I also really wanted, in my PHD

00:34:40.880 --> 00:34:44.090
I worked for cells that grow in culture

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:48.712
and I really wanted to
look at the actual organism

00:34:48.712 --> 00:34:52.950
I did work with mice
a little bit in my PHD

00:34:52.950 --> 00:34:55.970
